<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ServiceNow Update Set record-definition for a `sys_rate_limit_rules`
    record inside the scoped application `x_casemgmt` (Case Management).

    PURPOSE (review finding F07; AAP Sections 0.4.3, 0.7.2, 0.7.4):

      A PLATFORM-NATIVE per-hour request ceiling on the unauthenticated
      caller of the anonymous case-status-lookup endpoint

          GET /api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup?number=<CASE0000001>

      declared by ./sys_ws_definition_x_casemgmt_case_status_lookup.xml
      (base URI /api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup) and handled by
      ./sys_ws_operation_x_casemgmt_case_status_lookup_get.xml.

      WHY THIS ENDPOINT NEEDS A CEILING AT ALL. AAP Section 0.7.4 fixes
      the lookup contract as single-factor: a case number alone returns
      that case's status, subject and opened date to an anonymous caller,
      and case numbers are sequential (CASE0000001 upward, per
      ../../numbers/sys_number_x_casemgmt_case.xml). "HTTP 200 for a real
      number, HTTP 404 for an unused one" is therefore inherently a
      hit/miss oracle over a predictable sequence - review finding F07.
      The oracle cannot be removed without a second factor the AAP does
      not define and the Section 0.7.2 Minimal-Change Clause forbids
      inventing, so what remains is to make a sweep expensive, bounded and
      visible.

      This rule is the DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH layer of that answer. The primary,
      shipped defences are in script, in
      ../../script_includes/x_casemgmt_CasePortalService.xml:

        1. strict ^CASE[0-9]{7}$ validation before any query, so a
           malformed sweep reaches no database and learns nothing;
        2. a per-session sliding-window request ceiling held in the
           session's own client-data store, answered as HTTP 429;
        3. gs.warn abuse monitoring under the stable marker
           X_CASEMGMT_PORTAL_LOOKUP_ABUSE| carrying the client IP and the
           miss/total counts, so an operator can alert on a sweep.

      This record covers what those three cannot: a caller that DISCARDS
      ITS SESSION COOKIE between requests gets a fresh session, and
      therefore a fresh per-session window, every time. A per-hour ceiling
      on the unauthenticated user is not session-scoped, so it bounds that
      caller too.

    HONESTY ABOUT WHAT IS PROVEN (required, and deliberate):

      THE EFFECTIVENESS OF THIS RULE IS NOT VERIFIED. It was authored from
      the shipped REST records and from the shape of the 30 stock rows
      readable on the development instance; it has NOT been exercised
      against a live anonymous caller, because the instance is
      deliberately empty at the end of this run and no writes were made to
      it. Unproven in particular: whether the platform applies a
      rate-limit rule OWNED BY A SCOPED APPLICATION to an inbound
      anonymous request on a scoped scripted REST resource, and whether
      `api` / `api_resource` as spelled below bind to this application's
      REST resource after commit (both are choice fields the platform
      populates from its own REST registry).

      VERIFY POST-COMMIT, in this order:

        1. The rule installed and is active, and its resource bound:
             GET /api/now/table/sys_rate_limit_rules
                 ?sysparm_query=sys_id=55e38d54edf281eb97d455a75b11ac93
                 &sysparm_fields=name,api,api_resource,rest_api_resource,type,user,requests,active
           Expect one row, active=true, and api_resource exactly
           "GET /x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup". An empty or altered
           api_resource means the choice did not bind and the rule is
           inert - fix it on the form, where the choice list is populated
           from the live REST registry, and re-export.

        2. The platform is counting real anonymous traffic against it -
           the actual effectiveness check, which needs one anonymous GET
           to have happened first:
             GET /api/now/table/sys_rate_limit_count
                 ?sysparm_query=rate_limit_rule=55e38d54edf281eb97d455a75b11ac93
                 &sysparm_fields=user,request_count,count_start
           Expect a row whose `user` is the guest user and whose
           request_count tracks the anonymous lookups made. NO ROW AFTER A
           SUCCESSFUL ANONYMOUS GET MEANS THIS RULE IS NOT IN EFFECT,
           whatever step 1 showed.

        3. Refusals, once the ceiling is crossed:
             GET /api/now/table/sys_rate_limit_violations
                 ?sysparm_query=rate_limit_rule=55e38d54edf281eb97d455a75b11ac93
                 &sysparm_fields=user,sys_created_on

      Until step 2 returns a row, treat the in-script controls as the ONLY
      effective ceiling on this endpoint. That is why none of them is
      weakened on the strength of this file, and why
      ../../docs/portal-pages.md records this rule as unverified and keeps
      the residual enumeration risk stated rather than closed.

    THE CEILING, AND WHY THIS NUMBER:

      <requests>240</requests> - the column is "Request limit per hour"
      (sys_dictionary: sys_rate_limit_rules.requests, integer), so this is
      240 anonymous lookups per hour for the guest user across the whole
      instance.

      What it costs a legitimate requester: nothing measurable. A
      requester checking one case makes one request; the confirmation
      link's prefill makes one; a mistyped number adds another. The ATF
      suite makes a handful per run
      (../../atf/x_casemgmt_atf_19_portal_contract_lookup_of_a_valid_number_returns_only_status_subject_o.xml
      and
      ../../atf/x_casemgmt_atf_20_portal_contract_lookup_of_an_unknown_number_returns_404_with_the_verba.xml).

      What it costs a sweeper: the number space is CASE0000001 through
      CASE9999999, so at 240 numbers per hour an exhaustive sweep of it
      takes on the order of 41,600 hours - about four and a half years of
      uninterrupted requests to enumerate a space this POC populates with
      ten cases. That is the point of the number: it does not make the
      oracle disappear, it makes walking the sequence useless while
      leaving normal use untouched.

      Deliberately looser than the sibling submit rule
      (./sys_rate_limit_rules_x_casemgmt_case_submit.xml, 60/hour): this
      endpoint is a READ that persists nothing, while every refused write
      on the submit path still consumes a case number.

    WHY type=User AND A LITERAL GUEST sys_id:

      `type` is a choice with exactly three values on this release - All,
      Role, User (sys_choice on sys_rate_limit_rules.type). The caller to
      be bounded is UNAUTHENTICATED, so it holds no role and `Role` cannot
      address it; `All` would bound every caller of the resource including
      internal ones. `User` pointed at the platform's stock `guest` user
      addresses exactly the unauthenticated caller, which is who reaches
      this endpoint (the operation and its definition both set
      requires_authentication=false, so the platform serves the request as
      guest).

      <user> therefore carries 5136503cc611227c0183e96598c4f706, the stock
      guest user's sys_id, verified on the development instance by a
      read-only lookup of sys_user?user_name=guest. That literal VIOLATES
      AAP Section 0.7.2's no-hardcoded-sys_id constraint, and this file
      claims no exception to it. No override in force supersedes Section
      0.7.2, and nothing written here may narrow it.

      It is one more instance of the capability gap that Section 0.7.2
      cannot be met in this transport at all - reported, and counted with
      the rest, in ../../docs/PDI_LIMITATIONS_AND_KNOWN_ISSUES.md Section
      0.CR1.2. An Update Set payload field holds a literal value; there is
      no script layer inside it in which a `user_name=guest` lookup could
      run; and the platform offers no by-key form of a reference field.
      What follows bounds the damage and is mitigation, NOT authorization:

        - 5136503cc611227c0183e96598c4f706 is the platform's own stock
          guest record and carries that same sys_id on every ServiceNow
          instance, so unlike an instance-specific identifier it does not
          break on a fresh PDI.
        - display_value="guest" carries the human-readable key alongside
          it, so a reader - and a maintainer re-pointing the rule - never
          has to resolve the hex by hand.
        - An unresolved reference makes the rule inert rather than wrong:
          the ceiling would then apply to nobody, which the post-commit
          verification prescribed above detects.

      The human decision recorded in Section 0.CR1.2 for Section 0.7.2 as
      a whole settles this row along with it.

      If a future maintainer prefers this repo's key-in-the-text-node
      convention (as used by
      ../../seed-data/role_assignments/sys_user_has_role_x_casemgmt_demo_agent.xml),
      the correct change is to re-point the rule on the form and
      re-export - not to hand-edit the text node, because an unresolved
      `user` silently makes the rule apply to nobody.

    SCOPE AND TRANSPORTABILITY:

      sys_rate_limit_rules extends sys_metadata (verified read-only:
      sys_db_object.super_class -> sys_metadata), so it is an application
      file: it carries sys_scope / sys_package / sys_update_name, is owned
      by this scoped application, and travels inside the Update Set with
      the rest of the package. Zero global-scope artifacts are introduced
      by this file - <sys_scope> and <sys_package> both resolve to
      x_casemgmt (AAP Section 0.7.2 scoped-namespace-only constraint), and
      no ACL, business rule, property, email configuration or Store
      dependency is created.

    DERIVATION OF api / api_resource / rest_api_resource:

      Taken from the shipped REST records themselves, not invented:
        - `api` = "Case Status Lookup" - the <name> of
          ./sys_ws_definition_x_casemgmt_case_status_lookup.xml.
        - `api_resource` and `rest_api_resource` =
          "GET /x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup" - the stock rows on this
          instance format the resource as
          "<METHOD> <path with the leading /api removed>" (e.g.
          "GET /now/documents/explore" for an operation whose
          default_operation_uri is /api/now/documents/explore). The lookup
          operation's <default_operation_uri> is
          /api/x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup and its <http_method> is GET,
          giving the value below.
        - `version` = "latest" - the definition carries no versioned
          namespace and the operation's <web_service_version/> is empty,
          which is the same shape as the stock rows that read "latest".

    AUTHORED-ARTIFACT CONVENTIONS (matching every sibling in this package):

      - <unload> root with a single <record_update table="..."> child.
      - Inner element <sys_rate_limit_rules action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE"> for
        idempotent re-import (AAP Section 0.7.1 round-trip verification).
      - Elements ordered alphabetically, four-space indentation.
      - Stable audit fields (admin / 2025-01-01 00:00:00) so re-generating
        this file is byte-identical.
      - <sys_update_name>sys_rate_limit_rules_<sys_id></sys_update_name> -
        the format the platform itself uses on this table (observed on all
        30 stock rows), which is why the update name embeds the sys_id
        rather than a readable slug.
      - THIS RECORD'S OWN <sys_id> is a fabricated 32-character lowercase
        hex value, derived deterministically as
        MD5("sys_rate_limit_rules_x_casemgmt_case_status_lookup") =
        55e38d54edf281eb97d455a75b11ac93 so that re-generation is stable
        and a re-import updates the same row instead of creating a second
        one. A repository-wide grep for this value returned no other
        occurrence, so it collides with nothing shipped.
      - No PII: the only identifier in the file is the platform's own
        guest user, and no requester name, address or case content
        appears anywhere.

    LOAD ORDER (AAP Section 0.5.2):

      MUST load AFTER:
        - ../../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (the scoped
          application record - the sole scope authority in this package,
          so <sys_scope> and <sys_package> both resolve against it)
        - ./sys_ws_definition_x_casemgmt_case_status_lookup.xml and
          ./sys_ws_operation_x_casemgmt_case_status_lookup_get.xml (this
          rule names their API and resource; the rule installs regardless,
          but its choice values only mean anything once the resource they
          describe is registered)

      Nothing depends on this record: it constrains an existing resource
      and is referenced by no other artifact.
-->
<unload unload_date="2025-01-01 00:00:00">
<record_update table="sys_rate_limit_rules">
    <sys_rate_limit_rules action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE">
        <active>true</active>
        <api>Case Status Lookup</api>
        <api_resource>GET /x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup</api_resource>
        <import_set_table/>
        <name>x_casemgmt anonymous case status lookup (guest)</name>
        <requests>240</requests>
        <rest_api_resource>GET /x_casemgmt/case_status_lookup</rest_api_resource>
        <role/>
        <sys_class_name>sys_rate_limit_rules</sys_class_name>
        <sys_created_by>admin</sys_created_by>
        <sys_created_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_created_on>
        <sys_id>55e38d54edf281eb97d455a75b11ac93</sys_id>
        <sys_mod_count>0</sys_mod_count>
        <sys_name>x_casemgmt anonymous case status lookup (guest)</sys_name>
        <sys_package display_value="Case Management" source="x_casemgmt">x_casemgmt</sys_package>
        <sys_policy/>
        <sys_scope display_value="Case Management">82b99028936f74320d74d6f88357a5af</sys_scope>
        <sys_update_name>sys_rate_limit_rules_55e38d54edf281eb97d455a75b11ac93</sys_update_name>
        <sys_updated_by>admin</sys_updated_by>
        <sys_updated_on>2025-01-01 00:00:00</sys_updated_on>
        <table/>
        <type>User</type>
        <user display_value="guest">5136503cc611227c0183e96598c4f706</user>
        <version>latest</version>
    </sys_rate_limit_rules>
</record_update>
</unload>
